Step-by-step algorithm to rotate a linked list to the right by k positions:
Step 1: If the list is empty, has only one node, or k is 0, return the head as it is.
Step 2: Initialize a variable leng to 1.
Step 3: Set a pointer tail to the head.
Step 4: Traverse the list until you reach the last node.
•	Sub-step 4a: Move tail to the next node.
•	Sub-step 4b: Increase leng by 1 for each move.
Step 5: After reaching the last node, connect it to the head.
•	This makes the list circular.
Step 6: Calculate effective rotations using k % leng.
•	This avoids unnecessary full rotations.
Step 7: Calculate the number of steps to move from the head to reach the new tail:
•	frot_moves = leng - (k % leng) - 1
Step 8: Move the head pointer forward frot_moves times.
•	After this loop, the current head will be the new tail.
Step 9: Store head.next in a variable called newhead.
•	This will be the new head of the rotated list.
Step 10: Break the circular link by setting head.next = null.
Step 11: Return newhead.
Time Complexity:
The list is traversed once to find length and once to find the new head.
Time Complexity = O(n)
Space Complexity:
Only a few pointers and variables are used.
Space Complexity = O(1)



    ------------> Code <---------------

class Solution {
    public ListNode rotateRight(ListNode head, int k) {
        if(head==null || head.next==null || k==0)return head;

        int leng=1;
        ListNode tail=head;

        while(tail.next!=null){
            tail=tail.next;
            leng++;
        }
        tail.next=head;

        int frot_moves=leng-(k%leng)-1;

        for(int i=0;i<frot_moves;i++){
            head=head.next;
        }
        ListNode newhead=head.next;
        head.next=null;

        return newhead;
    }
}
